<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaqut al-Hamawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1179–<a href="/years/1229">1229</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Yaqut ibn Abdallah al-Rumi al-Hamawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaqut al-Hamawi was an Arabic geographer and biographer of Byzantine Greek birth who was enslaved as a boy, educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and later freed. Travelling widely as a merchant and copyist, he compiled the Mu'jam al-Buldan, a great alphabetical dictionary of places, and a dictionary of men of letters. His works preserve much information on towns of the Islamic world before the Mongol invasions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yaqut, meaning ruby or hyacinth, was the kunya of Ibn Abdullah, &quot;son of Abdullah&quot;. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the Byzantine capital called al-Rum in Arabic, whence his nisba al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>. Captured in war and enslaved, he became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a> of Askar ibn Abi Nasr al-Hamawi, a trader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, from whom he received the laqab al-Hamawi. As Askar's apprentice he learned accounting and commerce and travelled as his envoy, twice or three times, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. In 1194 Askar stopped his salary over some dispute, and Yaqut worked as a copyist and studied under the grammarian al-Ukbari. Five years later he made another mission to Kish for Askar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his return to Baghdad Yaqut set up as a bookseller and began to write. He then spent ten years travelling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His importance as a scholar lies in his testimony to the great, largely lost literary heritage held in libraries east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, since he was one of the last visitors before the Mongol invaders destroyed them. He gained much material from the libraries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, where he had studied for two years, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. About 1222 he was working on his Geography in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and he completed the first draft in 1224. In 1227 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and he moved from there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, where he died in 1229.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Mu'jam al-Buldan, &quot;Dictionary of Countries&quot;, was composed between 1224 and 1228 and completed a year before his death. It is classed as a literary geography: an alphabetical index of place names from the literary corpus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, with their vocalisations, their Arabic or foreign derivation and their location. Yaqut supplements the geographic descriptions with historical and ethnographic material, accounts of Muslim conquests, names of governors, monuments and local celebrities, and so preserves much early literary, historical, biographical and geographic material, in prose and poetry.</p><p>His Mu'jam al-Udaba, the &quot;Literary Encyclopedia&quot; of 1226, is also known as the Irshad al-Arib. Another work, al-Mushtarak, is a lexicon of geographical homonyms, edited by Ferdinand Wüstenfeld in 1846. Wüstenfeld also edited the Mu'jam al-Buldan in six volumes, published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yaqut al-Hamawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geographers such as <b>Yaqut</b> describe its markets and its rulers' wealth, but its internal history is poorly recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-kish">Emirate of Kish</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1211" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yaqut al-Hamawi"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1211">What was here in 1211? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
